The experiment employed a 6 GeV/c proton beam to generate an intense source of fast kaons ($\gamma_K \approx 60$) within the primary vertex region of the NA62 beamline. The facility featured a specialized detector optimized for this rare process, incorporating a 400-meter decay volume equipped with high-resolution tracking and calorimetry systems. Advanced trigger mechanisms, built upon the DAQ/DAE infrastructure, enabled the identification of decay signatures with low background levels. Data analysis relied on a sophisticated simulation framework to model detector response and background distributions, allowing for the precise determination of the signal yield. The results are discussed in the context of theoretical predictions and potential new physics implications.
